      "source": [
        "The Age Band \"At Risk\"\n",
        "Winner: **55+**\n",
        "\n",
        "Why: This segment has an Average Arrears Rate of **29.06%,** which is nearly 4x higher **than** any other group. Their Collection Efficiency is only **71.3%,** meaning nearly **30%** of what is owed by this group remains unpaid.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. The Age Band with \"More Arrears\"\n",
        "Winner: **55+**\n",
        "\n",
        "Why: With **999,929,599 KES **in total arrears, this group holds almost the entire debt of the portfolio. This is driven by both a high volume of loans** (18,105)** and a very high individual delinquency rate.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. The Age Band that is \"Actively Paying\"\n",
        "Winner:** 18-25**\n",
        "\n",
        "Why: This group is your most reliable payer. They have the highest Collection Efficiency at **95.73%** and the lowest Arrears Rate at **5.46%.** Despite being young, they are meeting their financial obligations much more consistently than the older segments."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3hyZb_67NY6K"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Here is why these trends matter operationally:**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Resource Allocation in Collections**\n",
        "  As the Arrears Rate climbed toward **28%,** the volume of accounts requiring manual intervention grew significantly.\n",
        "\n",
        "   The Operational Strain: If the collections team is static while the number of overdue loans doubles, each agent has less time to spend on high-value \"rehabilitation\" calls.\n",
        "\n",
        "   The Strategy: This data justifies a shift from \"calling everyone\" to predictive dialing. By focusing on the 55+ age segment (the highest risk/volume group), the team can recover more KES per hour worked.\n",
        "\n",
        "**2.Credit Scorecard & Onboarding Friction**\n",
        "   The FPD Rate (First Payment Default) started at a staggering **42.1%. **Operationally, this means nearly half of the customers were failing at the very first step.\n",
        "\n",
        "   The Bottleneck: High FPD rates lead to high Return rates. Repossessing, refurbishing, and restocking a phone costs significantly more than keeping a customer active.\n",
        "\n",
        "  The Strategy: The steady improvement of FPD (down to **31.7%)** suggests that the \"Credit Check Done\" process is becoming more effective. Operationally, MoPhones should continue to tighten the \"Top of Funnel\" requirements, perhaps by increasing deposit amounts for the riskiest demographic segments identified in your analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Inventory & Liquidity Management**\n",
        "  Credit performance is the \"gasoline\" for the circular economy model.\n",
        "\n",
        " The Cash Flow Gap: When Collection Efficiency drops (**from 75% to 72%)**, the company has less cash on hand to purchase new refurbished inventory.\n",
        "\n",
        "  The Operational Risk: If cash is tied up in \"Arrears,\" the procurement team cannot buy the next 5,000 devices. This creates a growth ceiling where the company cannot scale even if demand is high because the capital is \"trapped\" in unpaid phones.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. **Data Integrity & System Trust**\n",
        "  The \"Paid Off Paradox\" (accounts marked Paid Off but still showing Arrears) is an operational nightmare.\n",
        "\n",
        "  Customer Friction: If a customer believes they have finished their payments but the automated system still sends them collection SMS or locks their phone, NPS will plummet.\n",
        "\n",
        " The Strategy: This requires an immediate Operational Audit of the \"Status Change\" triggers."
      ]
    },
