      "source": [
        "summary of the NPS (Net Promoter Score) analysis and how it connects to credit risk:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Customer Mood\n",
        "Promoters (Happy):** 5,150**\n",
        "Detractors (Unhappy):** 4,737**\n",
        "\n",
        "Passives (Neutral):** 2,453**\n",
        "\n",
        "Note: A huge number of customers **(over 60,000)** have not given a score yet.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Why are customers unhappy?\n",
        "The Problem: There is a direct link between debt and sadness. Customers who had their phones returned or cancelled within the first 3 months are the most unhappy **(over 75% are Detractors).**\n",
        "\n",
        "The Surprise: Interestingly, some customers who \"Paid Off\" or had a \"Write Off\" (debt cleared) are actually quite happy. This shows that resolving the debt—one way or another—improves the mood.\n",
        "\n",
        "The Trend: As a customer falls further behind on payments (Days Past Due), their satisfaction score drops.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. The \"Tension\"\n",
        "There is a tug-of-war between Collecting Money and Keeping Customers Happy.\n",
        "\n",
        "If collections are too aggressive, we get our money back but lose the customer's trust forever.\n",
        "\n",
        "The \"Paid Off Paradox\" (where people think they finished paying but still get debt alerts) is a major reason why customers become Detractors.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. Recommendation: \"Help, Don't Just Punish\"\n",
        "Action: Start a Customer Rehabilitation Program for people who are only 1–30 days late.\n",
        "\n",
        "How: Instead of just demanding money, use \"empathetic communication.\" Offer flexible payment plans and ask why they missed a payment.\n",
        "\n",
        "Goal: Stop a \"Passive\" customer from becoming a \"Detractor.\" If we help them early, they are more likely to stay with MoPhones in the long run."
      ]
